1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,755
A mountain pass near the Indus, in the winter
of the year one thousand and forty. A

2
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:10,352
blind man is led out among a crowd of
soldiers. Ten years ago, he was a king.

3
00:00:10,352 --> 00:00:12,740
Tonight, they want him to be king again.

4
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:27,972
Welcome to The Honest Historian. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani, and this is Folio Three. Last

5
00:00:27,972 --> 00:00:32,975
week, a young Sultan woke up in his camp to
find that his army had gone over to his

6
00:00:32,975 --> 00:00:36,170
brother. By evening, he was a prisoner in a
fortress.

7
00:00:36,950 --> 00:00:41,798
Today, I want to climb up to that fortress and
ask a simple question. What happened

8
00:00:41,798 --> 00:00:46,471
to him? Because the answer is one of the
strangest turns of fortune in the whole

9
00:00:46,471 --> 00:00:47,990
history of the Ghaznavids.

10
00:01:03,270 --> 00:01:07,856
Let me remind you who he was. His name was
Muhammad, the younger son of Sultan

11
00:01:07,856 --> 00:01:12,383
Mahmud of Ghazni. Mahmud ruled a vast empire
from his capital, Ghazni, in the

12
00:01:12,383 --> 00:01:17,146
mountains of what is now Afghanistan. Before
he died in the year one thousand and

13
00:01:17,146 --> 00:01:20,850
thirty, he chose Muhammad as his heir, not his
elder son Masud.

14
00:01:21,630 --> 00:01:26,775
So for about five months, Muhammad was Sultan.
Then the generals changed sides, and

15
00:01:26,775 --> 00:01:31,859
Masud took the throne. Muhammad was taken to a
fortress near Tiginabad, in the dry

16
00:01:31,859 --> 00:01:36,570
south of Afghanistan, with his family and his
servants, and put under guard.

17
00:01:37,350 --> 00:01:41,812
Beyhaqi gives us a few precious glimpses of
him there. For two or three days, he sat

18
00:01:41,812 --> 00:01:46,008
in silence, lost in sorrow. When a letter came
from his brother, telling him he

19
00:01:46,008 --> 00:01:50,470
would stay in the fortress, honorably, but
under guard, he grew troubled. And an old

20
00:01:50,470 --> 00:01:53,710
official told him gently that what was meant
to be, had been.

21
00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:59,216
That was where we left him. A young man in his
early thirties, the son of the most

22
00:01:59,216 --> 00:02:03,999
famous king of his age, sitting behind stone
walls, while the world went on without

23
00:02:03,999 --> 00:02:04,230
him.

24
00:02:19,510 --> 00:02:24,890
What happened next is not easy to tell, and I
want to be honest with you about why.

25
00:02:25,670 --> 00:02:30,845
The History of Beyhaqi was once enormous. It
filled some thirty volumes. Only a part

26
00:02:30,845 --> 00:02:35,897
of it survives, the part that covers the reign
of Masud. Beyhaqi does not stop his

27
00:02:35,897 --> 00:02:38,670
story to follow a prisoner from year to year.

28
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:44,053
So for much of Muhammad's life after this, we
have only scattered lines, in Beyhaqi

29
00:02:44,053 --> 00:02:45,590
and in other chroniclers.

30
00:02:46,370 --> 00:02:50,944
Here is what most of them agree on. Muhammad
was blinded. In that world, blinding

31
00:02:50,944 --> 00:02:55,405
was a cruel but common way of making a prince
harmless. A blind man, by custom,

32
00:02:55,405 --> 00:02:59,922
could not rule. When exactly it was done, and
on whose order, the sources do not

33
00:02:59,922 --> 00:03:04,270
fully agree, and some modern scholars have
their doubts about the early date.

34
00:03:04,550 --> 00:03:09,150
But by the end of our story, every account
calls him the blind Muhammad.

35
00:03:09,930 --> 00:03:15,241
And so, for about ten years, he lived as a
prisoner. His sons grew up in captivity

36
00:03:15,241 --> 00:03:18,350
with him. Outside, his brother ruled the
empire.

37
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:37,950
Now let's step outside the fortress and look
at those ten years.

38
00:03:38,730 --> 00:03:43,420
They did not go well for Masud. He was brave
and generous, but he was also stubborn,

39
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:47,998
and he often refused good advice. In the north
and west of his empire, a new power

40
00:03:47,998 --> 00:03:52,632
was rising. These were the Seljuqs, Turkish
nomads from the steppes, who moved with

41
00:03:52,632 --> 00:03:55,870
their herds and their horsemen into the lands
of Khorasan.

42
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:01,207
At first, they seemed like a nuisance. Then
they became a danger. And in the year

43
00:04:01,207 --> 00:04:05,877
one thousand and forty, at a place called
Dandanqan, in the desert near the city of

44
00:04:05,877 --> 00:04:10,547
Merv, Masud's great army met them in battle
and was broken. We will tell that story

45
00:04:10,547 --> 00:04:12,010
properly in a later folio.

46
00:04:12,290 --> 00:04:17,446
For now, all you need to know is this: after
Dandanqan, the western half of the

47
00:04:17,446 --> 00:04:18,490
empire was lost.

48
00:04:19,270 --> 00:04:23,946
Masud went back to Ghazni. He was shaken, and
he did not trust the men around him.

49
00:04:23,946 --> 00:04:28,622
And he decided to do something that alarmed
his whole court. He would leave Ghazni

50
00:04:28,622 --> 00:04:33,355
and go to India, to the lands his family held
across the Indus River, to gather new

51
00:04:33,355 --> 00:04:34,210
strength there.

52
00:04:34,990 --> 00:04:39,744
His ministers begged him to stay. Beyhaqi, who
was in the royal chancery at the

53
00:04:39,744 --> 00:04:44,678
time, lets us feel how uneasy the court was.
But Masud would not listen. He loaded

54
00:04:44,678 --> 00:04:49,010
up his treasury, his family and his household,
and set off toward India.

55
00:05:04,290 --> 00:05:07,810
And here is the detail that changes
everything.

56
00:05:08,590 --> 00:05:13,509
Masud did not leave his blind brother behind.
According to the later chroniclers, he

57
00:05:13,509 --> 00:05:18,427
took Muhammad with him on the journey,
together with Muhammad's sons. Perhaps he was

58
00:05:18,427 --> 00:05:23,229
afraid that if Muhammad stayed in a fortress
near Ghazni, someone might use him as

59
00:05:23,229 --> 00:05:24,810
the banner for a rebellion.

60
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:29,010
If that was his fear, he took the danger with
him.

61
00:05:29,790 --> 00:05:34,159
The great caravan moved slowly through the
mountain passes, heavy with gold. At a

62
00:05:34,159 --> 00:05:38,473
pass called Marikala, near the Indus,
discipline collapsed. The soldiers and the

63
00:05:38,473 --> 00:05:42,950
palace slaves began to plunder the treasure.
And once they had done that, they knew

64
00:05:42,950 --> 00:05:45,970
there was no going back. Masud would never
forgive them.

65
00:05:46,250 --> 00:05:48,590
So they needed a new king.

66
00:05:49,370 --> 00:05:51,070
They went to Muhammad.

67
00:06:06,350 --> 00:06:11,474
Think about that moment. A blind man, who has
spent about ten years as a prisoner,

68
00:06:11,474 --> 00:06:14,910
is told by a crowd of soldiers that he is
Sultan again.

69
00:06:15,690 --> 00:06:20,151
It is said that he did not want it. Some of
the accounts suggest that he had to be

70
00:06:20,151 --> 00:06:24,503
pressed, even threatened, before he agreed.
Whether that was true reluctance, or

71
00:06:24,503 --> 00:06:29,072
simply wisdom, we cannot know. A blind king at
the head of a mutinous army was not a

72
00:06:29,072 --> 00:06:32,010
king for long in that world, and he may have
known it.

73
00:06:32,790 --> 00:06:37,826
But he accepted. And because he could not see
to govern, the real power went to his

74
00:06:37,826 --> 00:06:41,710
son Ahmad, a young man with his own anger and
his own ambitions.

75
00:06:42,490 --> 00:06:47,079
Masud, the Sultan who had once taken the
throne from his brother without a battle,

76
00:06:47,079 --> 00:06:51,723
now lost it to his brother in the same way. He
was seized and sent as a prisoner to

77
00:06:51,723 --> 00:06:56,368
a fortress called Giri, in what is now the
north-west of Pakistan. And there, early

78
00:06:56,368 --> 00:06:59,390
in the year one thousand and forty one, he was
killed.

79
00:06:59,670 --> 00:07:04,635
The chroniclers point mainly at Ahmad,
Muhammad's son, as the one who ordered it.

80
00:07:04,635 --> 00:07:07,270
Some say the order came in Muhammad's name.

81
00:07:08,050 --> 00:07:11,550
Two brothers. Two fortresses. Ten years apart.

82
00:07:26,830 --> 00:07:31,387
Now, at this point, I want to pause and tell
you something about Beyhaqi. Again and

83
00:07:31,387 --> 00:07:35,943
again in his book, he warns his readers about
this world, and how quickly it turns.

84
00:07:35,943 --> 00:07:40,445
In one famous passage, he writes a line that
every student of Persian prose knows.

85
00:07:40,445 --> 00:07:42,970
Let me say it in Persian, and then in English.

86
00:07:43,750 --> 00:07:49,910
Ahmaq marda ke del dar in jahan bandad, ke
nemati bedahad va zesht baz setanad.

87
00:07:50,690 --> 00:07:55,568
Foolish is the man who binds his heart to this
world, for it gives a blessing, and

88
00:07:55,568 --> 00:07:57,590
then takes it back in an ugly way.

89
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:03,574
Beyhaqi wrote that about another man, on
another day. But I cannot think of a better

90
00:08:03,574 --> 00:08:05,990
line for the two sons of Sultan Mahmud.

91
00:08:21,270 --> 00:08:25,939
The story is not quite over. Masud had sons
too. One of them, Mawdud, was in the

92
00:08:25,939 --> 00:08:30,667
north, in the region of Balkh, when the news
reached him. He gathered an army and

93
00:08:30,667 --> 00:08:32,710
marched south to avenge his father.

94
00:08:33,490 --> 00:08:38,022
In the spring of the year one thousand and
forty one, Mawdud met the army of

95
00:08:38,022 --> 00:08:42,972
Muhammad and Ahmad near Nangarhar, in the east
of today's Afghanistan, not far from

96
00:08:42,972 --> 00:08:47,982
the modern city of Jalalabad. Muhammad's side
was defeated. And this time, there was

97
00:08:47,982 --> 00:08:49,950
no fortress and no second chance.

98
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:55,350
Muhammad was put to death, and so were his
sons, Ahmad among them. On the field of

99
00:08:55,350 --> 00:09:00,470
his victory, it is said, Mawdud founded a town
and called it Fathabad, the town of

100
00:09:00,470 --> 00:09:00,970
victory.

101
00:09:01,750 --> 00:09:06,730
So the blind king's second reign lasted only a
few months. Like his first.

102
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:26,890
What is the point of this sad story? Why does
Beyhaqi's world remember it?

103
00:09:27,670 --> 00:09:32,353
I think it is because it shows us something
that the great chronicles of kings often

104
00:09:32,353 --> 00:09:37,035
hide. A throne is not a safe place. It does
not belong to the man who sits on it. It

105
00:09:37,035 --> 00:09:41,384
belongs, for a moment, to whoever can hold it,
and the next moment it is gone.

106
00:09:41,384 --> 00:09:45,230
Muhammad was crowned, imprisoned, blinded,
crowned again, and killed.

107
00:09:45,510 --> 00:09:50,997
Masud was the winner of Folio Two and the
victim of Folio Three. Neither of them, in

108
00:09:50,997 --> 00:09:53,610
the end, was the master of his own fate.

109
00:09:54,390 --> 00:09:59,009
Beyhaqi did not watch these events from far
away. He was a secretary at Masud's

110
00:09:59,009 --> 00:10:03,862
court. He knew these men. And he chose, again
and again, to write about the cost of

111
00:10:03,862 --> 00:10:08,422
power as well as its glory. That is the
honesty that makes him worth reading a

112
00:10:08,422 --> 00:10:09,650
thousand years later.

113
00:10:10,430 --> 00:10:15,201
If there is one lesson here, it is this: be
careful whom you lock away, because

114
00:10:15,201 --> 00:10:16,530
fortune keeps the key.

115
00:10:31,810 --> 00:10:36,353
Next week, we step back two centuries, to the
court of the caliphs in Baghdad.

116
00:10:36,353 --> 00:10:41,187
Beyhaqi tells a story there about a proud
general named Afshin, a nobleman named Bu

117
00:10:41,187 --> 00:10:45,730
Dulaf, and a judge who risked everything to
save a life. It is one of the most

118
00:10:45,730 --> 00:10:47,710
gripping scenes in his whole book.

119
00:10:48,490 --> 00:10:53,238
If you enjoyed this story, follow the show so
you don't miss the next one, and share

120
00:10:53,238 --> 00:10:57,874
it with someone who loves history. I'm Mehdi
Rahmani. This episode was retold from

121
00:10:57,874 --> 00:11:02,622
the Persian original of the History of
Beyhaqi, and narrated with an AI rendering of

122
00:11:02,622 --> 00:11:04,770
my own voice. Thank you for listening.

